  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 129 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nThe mid-level ridging stays in place through the end of the work \nweek, which will keep the region warm and rain free. Changes then \nstart to appear into and through the weekend as the flow aloft turns \nmore southwesterly as the mid-level ridging nudges to the east and \ntroughing slowly progresses across the Southwestern CONUS. Humidity \nalso gradually increases from Friday through the weekend with the \nlow-level and surface flow returning to and increasing from out of \nthe southeast, helping to draw in the moisture off the Gulf. For the \nvast majority of the weekend, this would mainly just yield to more \ncloud cover, especially nocturnally into each morning. Any Rain \nchances (15-35%) are looking to hold off until early next week, with \nthe potential for a front dropping southward into the region once \nthe trough in the Desert Southwest starts to eject northeastward \ninto the Texas and Oklahoma panhandle region. The deterministic \nmedium range guidance remains a bit more bullish on this front than \nthe ensembles means. This is due to a cluster of several ensembles \nthat indicate the upper level pattern may not be conducive enough \nfor the full push of a front across the region. The NBM guidance \ndoes indicate a northerly wind shift into early Tuesday behind the \nfront but only marginal cooling in the wake of the boundary at this \ntime. This is quite a bit of a weaker signal in comparison to the \nfront last week. We'll continue to monitor, as this could change \nwith these early season fronts.",
